Publish an internal service so it is reachable at
https://<name>.<your published domain>.

The certificate is already arranged — one wildcard covers every
published name. The DNS record is yours: point <name>.<domain> at
this router in your own zone, and note that a private answer there
needs the zone listed in `olr dns set --private-domain`.

Examples:
  olr ingress add grafana --client nuc --port 3000
  olr ingress add nas --client synology --port 5001 --scheme https
  olr ingress add hello --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000
  olr ingress add mi --host 172.16.1.163 --port 80 --header 'Host: 172.16.1.163'

Usage:
  olr ingress add <name> [flags]

Flags:
      --allow-from stringArray      source address or CIDR allowed to reach a restricted service, repeatable
      --client string               client to publish, by its name in the client list
      --expose string               who may reach it: lan (default), public, or restricted
      --header stringArray          set an upstream request header as 'Name: value', repeatable
  -h, --help                        help for add
      --host string                 address or hostname to publish, for a target that is not a client
      --icon string                 custom icon: theSVG ID (thesvg:grafana) or PNG/JPEG/WebP data URL; empty uses the site favicon
      --link-path string            page path to open and use for its icon (e.g. /ui); does not change proxy routing
      --port uint16                 port the service listens on
      --remove-header stringArray   remove an upstream request header, repeatable
      --scheme string               how to reach it: http, https (default http)

Global Flags:
      --dry-run         show what would change and exit without applying it
  -o, --output string   output format: text|json (default "text")
      --socket string   olrd control socket (default "/run/olr/olrd.sock")
